defmodule <%= inspect(@module) %> do
  @moduledoc """
  The example's context: how a page records an entry as the person signed in. It is the pages'
  door onto `<%= inspect(@contract) %>`, as `EhOsContract.Plug` is the door of a request to
  `/api`: it calls `EhOsContract.call/3` with the person's actor, so the engine asks whether they
  may before anything else (`<%= @contract_dir %>/calls.md`, "Call the contract from other
  code"). It sends
  the form's params as the form sent them, with `form: true`, and the engine reads them as
  `EhOsContract.Form` does (`<%= @contract_dir %>/forms.md`). No page builds the command's struct.

  ehos_app wrote it with the example contract, and it is the application's own to change or
  remove, with `<%= inspect(@live) %>`.
  """

<%= if @boundary do %>  # No `use Boundary` of its own: one here would take in the contract's decider,
  # `<%= inspect(@module) %>.Entry`, which the engine calls. It stays in the root's boundary,
  # which exports it to the web.

<% end %>  alias EhOsContract.{Actor, Receipt}

  @doc """
  Records an entry as `actor`, onto `entry_id`, the id the page gave the form: `params` as the
  form sent them, `"amount"` as text. The page decides the id, not the person: an `"entry_id"`
  in `params` is not read. The id is the idempotency key too, so a second call with it, once
  the first was accepted, answers the first's receipt and records nothing; a refused call can be
  made again. Refused `:missing_capability` when the actor's credential does not grant
  `<%= @name %>.record_entry`, whatever `params` hold.
  """
  @spec record_entry(Actor.t(), String.t(), map()) :: {:ok, Receipt.t()} | {:error, term()}
  def record_entry(%Actor{} = actor, entry_id, params) when is_binary(entry_id) do
    EhOsContract.call({:command, "<%= @name %>.record_entry"}, params,
      actor: actor,
      form: true,
      data: %{entry_id: entry_id},
      idempotency_key: "entry:#{entry_id}"
    )
  end
end
